  "full_text": "So I'm going to talk about my flight simulator and first-person shooter. First let's talk about GPU versus CPU. This is Mythbusters. The core of the graphics routines is the triangle fill. What it does is it calculates the slope and then it draws horizontal lines stepping down one row at a time um or it does vertical lines anyway so here's my flight simulator done with the cpu now we're gonna get some fish so let's look at the code for the flight simulator it's called eagle dive eagle dive so um first the graphics are right in the source code they're really big because uh i uh shrink them down anyway so um the The different cores, this is some documentation right in the source code. The different cores do strips going from back to front and they alternate. So one, two, three, four on the outside, five on the outside. And they're set up to alternate different so that you try to keep the cores from accessing the same locations. So this is a flight simulator done all the way from scratch. And here's my first-person shooter. It's done all the way from scratch. It's supposed to be a coffin. Making art is really tedious. God said they were dancing. Anyway, so we can, the core for the first-person shooter has graphics in source. These I don't shrink. Anyway, so it's about how many lines of code? First-person shooter is $597, and the flight simulator is, well, a lot of it is $1,000, $1,200.",
